{
  "content": "**Updated Cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the task spec from user messages only\nTreat the user\u2019s turns as the sole source of truth for the task spec. Do not import assumptions from assistant reasoning, partial derivations, or any answer key. If later user turns reveal missing details, merge them into the same evolving spec rather than replacing the task with a new interpretation.\n\n### 2) Reconstruct the full user intent incrementally\nIn sharded or multi-turn disclosures, accumulate user facts and constraints across turns, then reconcile them against the assistant\u2019s current plan at each step. Recheck earlier interpretations whenever new user information arrives; later turns can refine, narrow, or override earlier context. Do not freeze the spec too early.\n\n### 3) Use a constraint checklist, not a holistic impression\nTranslate every user-stated requirement into a checklist item, then compare the assistant\u2019s response against each item. This is better than judging overall quality because it catches omissions, substitutions, and partial matches. Missing even one user constraint is a meaningful divergence.\n\n### 4) Separate \u201caligned work\u201d from \u201cfinal correctness\u201d\nAn assistant can do several things right while still failing the user\u2019s actual request. Preserve only steps that truly match the user\u2019s goal, not steps that merely look plausible. Treat mathematically valid or internally consistent work as aligned only if it answers the exact user-specified target.\n\n### 5) Detect unsupported assumptions as divergence\nIf the assistant introduces an unstated condition, interpretation, simplification, or path, flag it even if the final answer later happens to be correct. Unsupported assumptions are often the first sign of drift and frequently explain later errors. The analyzer should distinguish user-supported inference from assistant-imposed structure.\n\n### 6) Check in two passes: missing constraints, then contradictions\nFirst ask: did the assistant use every user-stated constraint and requirement? Then ask: did it contradict, overwrite, or reinterpret any of them? This ordering helps separate incomplete progress from true mismatch. If the assistant simply hasn\u2019t used all disclosed information yet, that is not the same as using it incorrectly.\n\n### 7) Identify the first point of mismatch\nPrefer locating the earliest divergence in the trajectory, because early drift usually causes later failures and is more actionable than only describing the end result. When the assistant changes direction, determine whether that shift was justified by new user information or was just internal backtracking. Only the former is aligned.\n\n### 8) Evaluate against the information available at that moment\nIn sharded-disclosure settings, judge the assistant turn by turn using only the user information disclosed so far. Do not retroactively excuse an earlier mismatch because a later user turn happened to reveal something that could have made the earlier path workable. The analysis should reflect the state of the conversation at each point.\n\n### 9) Distinguish incomplete from incorrect\nIf the assistant has not yet incorporated all disclosed constraints, treat that as incomplete reasoning or partial alignment. If it has interpreted user facts wrongly, overwritten a requirement, or committed to an unsupported variant, treat that as a mismatch. This distinction matters because only the latter should drive corrective replacement.\n\n### 10) Keep the \u201cwhat looks right\u201d section narrow and durable\n\u201cWhat looks right\u201d should capture the stable core of the assistant\u2019s approach that remains valid under the full accumulated user spec, not just the final answer. Preserve reusable local alignment, but do not let superficially plausible steps hide the fact that the overall direction is wrong.\n\n### 11) Make \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d specific and trajectory-relevant\nPrioritize issues that would change the next response\u2019s direction: missing constraints, incorrect relations, wrong target quantity, or premature commitment to an unsupported interpretation. State what is wrong and what the correct target is, without over-prescribing how the assistant must fix it. Compact, concrete issue statements are most useful for correction.\n\n### 12) Separate different error types\nKeep arithmetic/semantic mistakes, wording mismatches, scope mismatches, and irrelevant extra reasoning distinct. A response can be wrong because it solves the wrong problem variant, because it omits a required constraint, or because it adds unsupported material; these should not be collapsed into one generic \u201cincorrect\u201d label. Only the truly incorrect reasoning should force context replacement.\n\n### 13) Preserve confirmed facts; remove misleading assumptions\nWhen preparing compacted context or S2-style cleanup, retain the verified user constraints and the assistant\u2019s confirmed-correct work, but remove incorrect intermediate assumptions that could anchor later analysis. The goal is to re-center on the user\u2019s actual request, not on the previous answer path. Avoid carrying forward reasoning that is merely plausible but unsupported.\n\n### 14) Treat later user turns as refinements, not separate problems\nLater disclosures often clarify or correct earlier statements. Reconcile them into one evolving specification instead of treating each turn as an independent task unless the user clearly changes the request. This helps catch constraint drift and prevents the analyzer from analyzing the wrong variant in isolation.\n\n### 15) Use the user spec as the reference point, not the ground truth as a substitute\nThe analyzer\u2019s job is to compare assistant behavior against what the user asked for, then note where it diverged. Ground truth can help interpret implied targets, but it should not become an alternate source of requirements. The relevant question is whether the assistant matched the user\u2019s stated intent and constraints.",
